        <p>Concert of Europe. The result as • gate el atfaiss which should rouge t'se indignatioe of all fair-minded people. On the mainland where the Cheeks are weakest the Turks are allowed a free heed and are, it in .ad, even aided by German strategists. Ate. the l;•reeks are handicapped at every turn. The most coveted prise to be won by the Greeks in a war againnt Turkey is Crete, yet the European powers—alwaym, be it remembered, including England -forbid° the Greeke fleet to come sear that island. Well may the Kayo of Gsresee exclaim that the whole of Europe is against him and his country. The responsibility Of England imder the preaent circumstances is particularly heavy. Thie country under the bold and wise leadership of CANNING Mee One of the mein instruments in securing the liberty of ireece. There is not a little danger that owing to the weaknens of Lord SALIMBURT all that was, them gained may now be font.</p>
        <p>Tunas is not just now a great deal mid publicly about the Onerrymen. Peerlayn quarrymen's dispute but there are many indications that the subject is in I the minds of • vast number of English people. - At ell evente,the organieed section of the working- ohmso mom to have thoroughly realised that the quarrymen are fighting thi• battle with plucky pertinacity, and that they deserve a steady and luiremitting support. The Miners'Federation haa so far contributed 11850 and this sum is added to every week. The London Society of Compositors has raised Li /if) and • great number of other trade unions have contributed a share to the fund in aid of the quarrymen which has now reached a total of over £13,000. But perhaps the moat sugges- tive of all the donation. is that of £l5O made by the National Union of Teachers on Wednesday. Tho Union line been in conference at Swansea this week and the subject was raised owing to the mtion of the executive in first deciding to propene a grant and then withdrawing the proposal. The discussion made it very clear upon which side in the atruggle the sympathies of the temthers are. Teachers have themselves in the past had to light for the right of combination and it is therefore not surprising that they have a fellow-feeling for the Bethesda quarrymen. Of course, there was some oppoei- Lion to the vote—it would hove been strange if there had not been in a society numbering nearly 40,0111 n member*, but the opposition was in a de- cided minority for a ballot result.' in 14,524 vote. being recorded in favour and 7,515 against, giving the substantial majority of 11,00 P. The scene when the result was made known wsst is reported, particularly animated as both male and female teachers exhibited their approval by cheering and waving their hands. It may be added that the teachers' contribution ilid not end with the MO as another .1:50 .1101 personally contributed.</p>
        <p>A correspondent calls attention to • rather singu- lar incident at the borough jubilee dinner a fort- night ago. It wax in connection with • tong which la usually honoured ia one form or another on these occasions—that of "The Bishops, Clergy and all ministers of religion. The toast was pro- posed by Mr. W. Long and responded to by the Rector on behalf of the "biahops and clergy." and by the Itev. J. Warnock an behalf of the other " Ministers of Religion:. As long as the Rector was speaking all the reverend gentlemen present : acknowledged a community of interest by standing, ! bat when the Rector had finished, the clergy dis- sociated themselves from the rent of the toast by ! taking their aeats. What is the cacao of this "new departure ?" That te a tomtit:a which has been asked but which can not be or easily answered. If the clergy were not, generally speaking, very intel- ligent gentlemen one might say that broach of good taste WS, due to wesk-headedneel rattier than wrong-hvadedness. Or, if the clergy were not ass rule models of the mildest urbanity one might as- cribe it to downright vileness. On the whole, pro- bably the meet reasonable explanation is that this It the latest clerical fad. If that he NO, it hi to be hoped that it will soon pave away, for it is emin- ently calculated to make the very estimable gentle- men who practise it 'nob -- and, no doubt, :eel rather foolish.</p>
        <p>It wan a surprise and an intense disappointment to everyone when flood rriday opened with quit* a gale of wind and heavy rain. The previous Loy bad been fine and dry, auguring well for an enjoy- able holiday. There wee no reason to anticipate mob • complete change, and oisny were the pro- grammes arranged for palming the day pleamntly and profitably. All thme were summarily dashed when morning broke with an outlook forbidding in the extreme. Towards noon the elementary condi- time underwent another change, and sunshine soon held complete away, though the wind still Weer strongly. Consequent upon the improvement of the weather the people visited favourite resorts in the:country in linodreds,and a really enjoyable limo was spent.</p>
        <p>The last trace of the disaster at Latchford Locks caused by the steamship " Harold," was removed on Mooday, when the work of replacing ths temporary steel gate. at the top of the large locks with the repaired greecheart strueurea wan accomplished in 12 hours. The two leaves of the gate. were in- jured in the collieion, but not sn seriously so the intermediate gate, thongh they nail to he telton to the gate-shed at The;wall and fitted with new mitre-posts. Per about sin months oteel gates have been useitand on Monday the repaired leaves weigh., log some 140 tons were 'hided down, set. Sal put in working order during ths space of 12 hours, which can enly be looked upon as • very smut performence indeed. Mr. W. 11. Hester (chief •ngineer of the Canal', superintended the work. On Cool Priday the oeighbourhood of the locks WI. o .Ml.O of conaidemble animation owing to the large number of Rightism. who arrived by wagon- iitt• or 0i1,.r of the •arlous mamas of conveyance •vailahl• nowadays.</p>
