        <p>On T Awl's"; =nl4.X;i-X;.c. 'MO. Jansen H. Taylor.eon of Mr. Sstnuel Taylor, J.P., C.C.. and chair- man at the Runcorn When District Council,whiLst riding his bicycle in High-Cote:. Mr. Toylor was enretng down Waterloo Bridge tower&amp; hie father'', office when his machine shidded through the wet condition of Inc road• And he who thrown violently to the ground. Mr. Taylor was found to bentunnei beside. having sustained mownl bruit"; atmut the face. Medical assietance VIS 4 procured end he was conveyed home. Calorie the day hewn able to leave the hon., and oppenred none the tom with the exception of • few wars on hio fate, for his nasty opill. n 190.11141, S•11.0.11._</p>
        <p>On Saturday morning, it Runcorn Police Court, two sailors mimed ['hams. William Phillipa and William Twohig, made their appearance before Sims. P. Speak- man and J. W. Lightburn, the former on a merge of being drunk and dimrderly in Peel-street on Thursday midnight, and the 140er with obstructin namable Upton whilst in the execution of his duty - the same time and place. The °direr eteed that he was called to the etreet named where he found Phillips drunk and creating a disturbance. lie declined to go away, and , afterwards airsick 4C witness several time, end as he, was being taken to the police station attempted to throw • him down. Twohlx came between thr officer and Phillip, end inide a,, .11,.rt to remit. hlin.—A line of 51. and 7a 4 1. awn man imp sed in each one, or seven Maya' imprisonment.</p>
